{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: the MCP outputSchema lane needs two things from you that I cannot do for myself — an amendment to 206708ca's verification condition, and a repair child. Codex filed changes-requested at 86791837 and I ratified it at 08fca44c without contesting any of the three blockers. A ratified changes-requested authorizes nothing, so I am asking rather than proceeding.\n\nFIRST, THE CONDITION, AND MY OWN MISSTEP IN HANDLING IT. 206708ca requires verification against a real client that structuredContent is now recognized. That condition cannot be met by anyone. Hugh researched the client that produced the original report and told us directly: the version of kiro he was using has no support for structuredContent at all, with or without an advertised outputSchema, and we do not know any environment that would positively confirm it. An MCP-process restart does not help either, because the adapters are built from main and this change is not in main.\n\nWhat I did wrong was to put that question to codex. I asked them, in the review request, to rule on whether the unsatisfiable condition should block. That was not a reviewer's decision to make. The condition belongs to your request; only you can amend or retire it; and a reviewer who waived it would be authorising the implementer's shortcut, which is the exact failure independent review exists to prevent. Codex refused correctly and said so plainly. I withdraw the question and I am putting it where it belongs.\n\nWhat I believe survives, for you to accept or reject: the motivating premise is disproved, so this change cannot be justified as repairing an observed breakage. The narrower justification is conformance and self-description — the MCP specification provides outputSchema for exactly this declaration, gitseq returns structuredContent while declaring nothing about its shape, and a client that gates on the schema's presence now has what it needs. Hugh's stated judgement is that the schema inclusion is worthwhile on those terms. If you agree, please amend or retire-and-refile 206708ca on that narrower standards objective and drop the real-client condition. If you disagree and think the row should simply close unbuilt, say that instead and I will retire the branch; I have no stake in the outcome.\n\nSECOND, THE REPAIR CHILD. Two defects at exact head 284f9002 are real and mine, independent of the condition question.\n\n  (1) A false statement in the test. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go says the negotiated protocol admits only an object output schema. That is false for MCP 2026-07-28, which defines outputSchema as JSON Schema generally. Object is right here for a narrower reason: every gitseq success payload encodes as an object, and object stays compatible with older protocol revisions. The source comment and the commit message also still carry the client-causation framing after hugh disproved it — I corrected that in the artifact and failed to carry the correction back into the tree, which is the worse half of the mistake, because the artifact is the thing a reader is least likely to consult.\n\n  (2) An untested load-bearing guard. The test asserts every tool carries an object-typed outputSchema, but never asserts the permissiveness. Changing additionalProperties from true to false keeps the test green while making ordinary non-empty structuredContent invalid against the schema being declared — so the mutation that would actually break clients passes silently. My own artifact called the permissiveness the deliberate heart of the design and then left it unpinned. This is the mutation discipline I have asked other implementers to apply to their guards, and I did not apply it to mine.\n\nThe repair is: recut the protocol claim around the verified shape and standards-based interoperability without presenting an observed repair; carry the corrected framing into the source comment and commit message; and either assert additionalProperties is true or validate representative non-empty responses against the declared schema. Codex also asks that the comments be shortened to the proved object invariant and the schema rationale, and I agree.\n\nSEQUENCING, so you do not have to work it out. Please file the child AFTER deciding the condition question, and let the child name the amended objective — otherwise I will implement against wording you are about to change. Note also that the existing implementing commit carries Rests-On: 206708ca in its message and a trailer is immutable, so if you retire-and-refile rather than amend in place, the successor must exist before I recut; retiring first strands the only work there is.\n\nMy promise 9c20ee34 stays live and I remain available. Nothing here is blocked on my capacity.","body":{"conditions":"Decide the unsatisfiable real-client verification condition in 206708ca: amend or retire-and-refile on the narrower conformance/self-description objective, or close the row unbuilt. Then file a repair child for exact head 284f9002 covering: (a) the false 'protocol admits only object' claim in cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go plus the stale client-causation framing in the source comment and commit message, and (b) the missing assertion that additionalProperties is true. File the child after the condition decision, and file any successor request before retiring 206708ca since the implementing commit's Rests-On trailer is immutable.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}